{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["עזי וזמרת יה — The strength and song of Israel's praise is YHWH, and He was to me for salvation. עזי — chase-kametz with extra yod, like רבתי בגוים, היושבי בשמים, שכני בחגוי סלע. But עֻזִּי אֵלֶיךָ אֶשְׁמְרָה — meaning 'my strength' that is mine." "וזמרת — like ושכורת ולא מיין, and likewise תסמר שערת בשרי. Doubling at the beginning of the verse, and a wind passing over will make the hair of my flesh bristle. So Rav Yosef translated: תצהלב שלהוביתא בשירי — see, זמרת is defective like שכורת and שערת." "זה אלי — Even though they did not see Him, it is still fitting to use the demonstrative 'this,' like כִּי זֶה מֹשֶׁה הָאִישׁ." "ואנוהו — (ואייפהו) like הַנּוֹוה וּמְעֹנָגָּה דְּמִיתִי בַּת צִיּוֹן. And it is not from the root of נוה משלח, for the doubling of its final letter proves it." "וארוממנהו ואנוהו — Both are language of honoring the Holy One, blessed be He."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "Rendered חטף קמץ as 'chase-kametz'; Rashbam's grammatical term for the short qamqatan vowel. The three biblical examples (Psalms 116:6, Isaiah 33:5, Psalms 27:5) all show an extraneous yod in a singular construct/pronominal suffix form.", 1 "Rashbam's logic: זמרת parallels שכורת and שערת — forms where a consonantal vav or yod is absent (defective spelling), and the suffix -ת functions grammatically the same way. The Aramaic targum line cited from Rav Yosef renders זמרת by the root צה״ל/ש״לה (to exult/praise). The middle clause about 'wind passing over' reads hyper-literally; the syntax is elliptical and possibly corrupt in the received text."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:41:39.200844518Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}